1: # Part 2 main argument: divisor meridians at the corner
2: 
3: This proof draft is independent of all earlier runs. It is under cross-check by the other agents in this run. The lattice and analytic constructions, not a previously asserted non-existence result, are its inputs.
4: 
5: ## P2.6. The actual sixfold parameter descent and its collar
6: 
7: Put b=4l1+3l2, a=b-12l0=-p. The full twist vectors may have delta components; they have no effect on the calculations below because the c-variation of Pi(v_j) is -12l_j v delta. Fix s=log(q)/(2pi i), v=log(r)/(2pi i), c=-12v. The period columns are those of P2.1. On the regular t-base define
8: 
9:     H(t)=-(4l1 log(t)+3l2 log(t-1))/(2pi i).
10: 
11: Its changes of branch are integral multiples of the period delta. Thus translation by H(t)delta is a well-defined holomorphic section of the torus group. The parameter deck v->v+1 acts on the regular-family coordinates by
12: 
13:     R:(t,v,zeta)->(t,v+1,zeta+H(t)delta).
14: 
15: This is the descent map for the family built with the prescribed finite gluings: near the first root disc the gluing changes by -4l1 log(t)/(2pi i)delta; the remaining term in H is holomorphic there. Near the second root disc the same assertion holds with -3l2 log(t-1). Adding this bounded holomorphic delta-translation extends the descent over the finite cap. Since c changes by -12, the finite affine action changes by -12l_j/m_j delta, an integral period, so the cap action itself is unchanged. This verifies the descent on both finite charts.
16: 
17: Near the cusp write H=b s+U(q), where U=-3l2 log(1-q)/(2pi i) is holomorphic at q=0. In the cusp chart eta=zeta+l0 z_gamma, the parameter descent is translation by (a s+U)delta. Adding the lattice period a gamma rewrites this as
18: 
19:     (6a mu, U+aC-12a v),
20: 
21: which is holomorphic and bounded in q at zero, for each v. Its exponentials are units in every toric chart. Therefore it extends over the cusp filling. This fixes the collar sign and constructs the actual descended family over r*, without using an isomorphism assertion from an earlier run.
22: 
23: ## P2.7. Explicit origins and their differences
24: 
25: On q=Q^12 define the following lifts of sections into C²:
26: 
27:     o=(-b mu s/2,
28:        b s²/24-b C s/12+l0 s/2+v(b s+U)),
29: 
30:     sigma=-l0 z_gamma+
31:        (6a mu v, vU+aCv-6av²+6av).
32: 
33: Their deck differences are
34: 
35:     o(s+12,v)-o(s,v)=-b gamma+6(b+l0)delta,
36:     o(s,v+1)-o(s,v)=H delta,
37:     sigma(s+12,v)-sigma(s,v)=-12l0 gamma+78l0 delta,
38:     sigma(s,v+1)-sigma(s,v)=H delta+a gamma.
39: 
40: Every extra term is a lattice period, so these are actual holomorphic sections of the descended torus torsor. In cusp coordinates sigma+l0 z_gamma is the displayed holomorphic function of q and v, and hence sigma extends over q=0 in the smooth open orbit of the cusp component. Subtracting o makes the r-deck pure: the coordinates eta=zeta-o have trivial parameter deck on the vector coordinate. The rank-one toroidal construction below extends eta=0 over r=0. Thus o is an extending origin for that partial filling.
41: 
42: Exact subtraction gives
43: 
44:     sigma-o=a F0,
45:     F0=(mu(s+12v)/2,
46:         C(s+12v)/12-(s+12v)²/24+6v).
47: 
48: After q=Q^(12m), r=R^n, its differences are
49: 
50:     Delta_Q F=a m gamma-6a m² delta,
51:     Delta_R F=a n gamma-6a n(n-1)delta.
52: 
53: In particular the radial coefficient is -pn, with the printed definition p=12l0-4l1-3l2 and F=sigma-o. The proposed +pn has the wrong sign in these conventions. The default p=-1 reproduces the F printed in the prompt.
54: 
55: The calculations are polynomial identities and are independently checked in code/part2_origins_and_group.py. The holomorphic functions mu,C,U are invariant under the integer s shifts used here; no numerical approximation occurs.
56: 
57: ## P2.8. The rank-one partial filling
58: 
59: We spell out the local toroidal chart used in P2.7. On a simply connected finite-base chart, first quotient the vector coordinates by the delta period and put W=exp(-2pi i eta2), z=eta1. The remaining periods act by
60: 
61:     (z,W)->(z+1,W),
62:     (z,W)->(z+tau,exp(-2pi i mu)W),
63:     (z,W)->(z+6mu,d(t)r^12 W),
64: 
65: where d is a nonzero holomorphic unit on the chosen finite-base chart. The first two transformations define a degree-zero holomorphic line bundle without its zero section over the elliptic curve C/(Z+tau Z); no product assertion is made about this bundle.
66: 
67: Use the infinite chain with node chart indexed by i in Z,
68: 
69:     X_i=W/r^i, Y_i=r^(i+1)/W, X_iY_i=r.
70: 
71: The overlap where Y_i is nonzero is given by X_(i+1)=Y_i^(-1), Y_(i+1)=rY_i. Together with the corresponding inverse overlap these are holomorphic isomorphisms, and give the standard infinite P1 chain at r=0. Units acting on W act inversely on X_i,Y_i and extend; the third period shifts i by twelve, translates z by 6mu, and acts by the unit d. The quotient therefore has a cycle of twelve P1 bundles over the elliptic base at r=0. The node charts are smooth because r=X_iY_i is a coordinate function on the smooth (X_i,Y_i)-plane; the extra elliptic and base coordinates give a smooth fourfold. The central divisor is reduced with normal crossings.
72: 
73: For properness, restrict to a relatively compact finite-base chart and sufficiently small r. The elliptic lattice has a compact fundamental parallelogram uniformly on that chart. The period shifting i by twelve gives twelve node neighborhoods and twelve middle annuli as a compact fundamental region in the chain; the unit d and the line-bundle transition units are bounded above and below on this compact base. The usual inner and outer radius bounds can therefore be chosen uniformly. These regions cover the quotient, and their closures are compact over a closed smaller r-disc. This proves properness. At nonzero r the quotient is exactly the displayed torus, so its chart comparison is explicit.
74: 
75: At the finite root point j, the affine translation v_j/m_j has gamma coefficient l_j/m_j. Its W multiplier contains r^(12l_j/m_j), so it rotates the component cycle by 12l_j/m_j. The shifts are 4l1 and 3l2. The affine generator has order m_j and every nontrivial power moves the twelve-cycle by a nonzero rotation: 3 does not divide l1 and l2 is odd. Thus the action is free also on the central divisor. Quotienting the smooth charts gives smooth total space, a reduced NC r-central divisor, and the original finite multiple fibre in t. The overlap maps are the explicit logarithmic gluing translations; after subtracting their local origins, their remaining factors are holomorphic units and integral chain shifts, so they extend in these charts.
76: 
77: The component cover on the regular t-base is
78: 
79:     y^12=t^(4l1)(t-1)^(3l2).
80: 
81: Its three rotations are 4l1,3l2,-b modulo12; the cusp rotation is also p modulo12. Gluing this finite-base filling to the prescribed q-cusp filling over r* by the translation sigma-o gives a smooth-total, proper partial filling over (P1 x Delta_r) minus (infinity,0). Properness and the Hausdorff property follow locally on this covered base; the two maps agree on the overlap by P2.7.
82: 
83: ## P2.9. Extension of the marked algebraic-reduction map over any proper filling
84: 
85: The marked map f of the (3,4) family extends meromorphically over every proper analytic filling of a finite parameter cover.
86: 
87: Proof. Take the reduced irreducible component of the total space that dominates the parameter disc, and resolve it isomorphically over the punctured disc. The result Z is smooth and proper over the disc. The relative canonical line bundle omega_(Z/Delta) restricts on every noncentral fibre to K_X. The direct chart computation P5.5 gives K_X^(-2)=f*O_P1(1), with h0=2. Consequently the coherent sheaf p_*omega^(-2) has rank two and no torsion: multiplication by the parameter is injective on a line bundle on this dominating component, hence also on its pushforward. A torsion-free coherent sheaf on a smooth disc is locally free. Choose its frame. Evaluation gives a meromorphic map Z to P1 agreeing on every noncentral fibre with f followed by a projective linear transformation. Resolving its graph makes F=(p,f') proper over P1 x Delta.
88: 
89: The relative critical locus is the closed analytic rank-drop locus of dF. Its image is analytic by the proper mapping theorem. Off zero its image has exactly three points in each P1, namely the two multiple fibres and the cusp. Thus its nonvertical components are three analytic curves of degree one over the punctured disc. Their multiplicity-three, multiplicity-four and cusp labels remove permutation monodromy. Their normalizations give three meromorphic sections of P1 over the whole disc, even when their limits coincide. The unique Möbius transformation sending them to 0,1,infinity has meromorphic coefficients (the elementary cross-ratio formula). Composing produces the desired meromorphic marked f. Resolve this last graph. Every modification made is an isomorphism over the punctured parameter disc, except harmless modifications within q=0 introduced later to make divisors NC. ∎
90: 
